# assert-before-any-request.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M87): an assertion with no response to read
# — demonstrates `tflw check`'s teaching diagnostic (TF039: checker, `checkResponseScopes`, tflw M87
# / review `A4-16`, `FU-12`).
#
# Two shapes here, and the second is the interesting one.
#
# `test "asserts before making any request"` is the plain case `A4-16` filed: `expect status` as a
# body's first step. Statically obvious, and it cost a whole run to discover.
#
# `test "asserts on an action's response"` is `FU-12`, and it looks correct. `fetch health()` really
# does perform an `api` step — but a response never leaves the body it was made in. tflw's
# interpreter keeps `lastResponse` per `execSteps` frame, and an `action`, a hook and each nested
# `within`/`switch to new tab`/`download` block all get their own. So the action's own `expect`
# passes, and the caller's next one has nothing to read. Same rule, applied where a reader's
# intuition says otherwise — which is why it is worth a fixture rather than a footnote.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/assert-before-any-request.tflw

action fetch health()
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200

test "asserts before making any request"
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.status as status

test "asserts on an action's response"
  fetch health()
  expect status equals 200
